     1	# REPO RULES
     2	
     3	Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory
     4	before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule
     5	file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it
     6	holds no rules of its own.
     7	
     8	---
     9	
    10	## 1. HOW TO USE THIS
    11	
    12	1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.
    13	2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.
    14	3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**
    15	4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more
    16	   specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an
    17	   edge case.
    18	5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.
    19	
    20	### Precedence
    21	
    22	| Level | Source | Can be overridden? |
    23	|-------|--------|--------------------|
    24	| 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |
    25	| 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |
    26	| 3 | These rule files | Only by level 1 or 2 |
    27	| 4 | General judgment | By anything above |
    28	
    29	A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what
    30	`AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,
    31	while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content
    32	is not.
    33	
    34	---
    35	
    36	## 2. TRIGGER TABLE
    37	
    38	| You are about to… | Load | It settles |
    39	|-------------------|------|-----------|
    40	| Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · generalize something that works · write "flexible", "future-proof", "scalable", "extensible", "might need", "best practice", "while we're here" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |
    41	| Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked | [`scope-discipline.md`](repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |
    42	| Say "done", "works", "fixed", "passing", "no regressions" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |
    43	| Hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |
    44	| Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |
    45	| Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |
    46	| Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |
    47	| Write any substantive reply · the reader says they did not follow | [`communication.md`](repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: length, filler, and no table in a reply |
    48	| Draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · choose a word · place a dash, colon or semicolon · split or join sentences | [`prose-mechanics.md`](repo-rules/prose-mechanics.md) | How a sentence reads: sentence and paragraph shape, plain words, punctuation |
    49	| Present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · answer a complex or ambiguous request · start a multi-step stretch the reader cannot see inside · report what a long run found · list every option you considered | [`presenting-decisions.md`](repo-rules/presenting-decisions.md) | Verdict first, one recommended path, saying where you are going before a long stretch |
    50	| End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`handoff-and-questions.md`](repo-rules/handoff-and-questions.md) | What is the operator's to do next, and when a question becomes a structured choice |
    51	| Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what "integrated" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |
    52	
    53	---
    54	
    55	## 3. INDEX
    56	
    57	| Rule | Summary |
    58	|------|---------|
    59	| [Overengineering](repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |
    60	| [Scope discipline](repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |
    61	| [Evidence and proof](repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |
    62	| [Delegation and orchestration](repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |
    63	| [Blast radius](repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |
    64	| [Root cause](repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |
    65	| [Uncertainty and honesty](repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |
    66	| [Communication](repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |
    67	| [Prose mechanics](repo-rules/prose-mechanics.md) | How a sentence reads: sentence and paragraph shape, plain words, punctuation. |
    68	| [Presenting decisions](repo-rules/presenting-decisions.md) | When the reader has to decide or act on what you found, lead with the verdict, recommend one path, and say where you are going before a long stretch of work. |
    69	| [Handoff and questions](repo-rules/handoff-and-questions.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |
    70	| [Hub routing](repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A parent hub projects one advisor identity and routes in two stages. A nested mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a per-hub gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |
    71	
    72	Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to
    73	permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.
    74	
    75	---
    76	
    77	## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT
    78	
    79	**In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,
    80	the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply
    81	reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control
    82	back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the
    83	second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose
    84	trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,
    85	which fires at every turn's end, and §8 keeps the two clauses that must bind even when
    86	nothing loads.
    87	
    88	**Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*
    89	of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.
    90	Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately
    91	absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and
    92	posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.
    93	
    94	**The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a
    95	route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is
    96	In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`
    97	is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:
    98	the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that
    99	admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.
   100	
   101	**The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that
   102	overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:
   103	naming a question tool per runtime reads as the "which command" family, and the paragraph
   104	above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,
   105	and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between
   106	runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that
   107	asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not
   108	a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records
   109	which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `handoff-and-questions.md`
   110	is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and
   111	it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between
   112	runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.

